{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffddgT2nfM9R8wbG7RKr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, and the admission self-check prints `OK`. On its short smoke fit the ELPD-LOO came out at −2237.6, against the incumbent's −2242.7 from its full fit. That's encouraging but not a fair comparison yet: the smoke run is 100 draws on one chain.\n\n**Model `tally_span_switch_ideal_triplet_variety`** is a refinement of the incumbent `tally_span_switch_rate_ideal_2`. It keeps everything the incumbent has:\n- each person's expected span of the running heads-minus-tails tally;\n- sensitivity that varies by person and scales with sequence length;\n- the shared ideal switching rate, weighted differently per person;\n- each person's left/right lean.\n\n**The one change:** a shared reward on the share of distinct three-flip chunks, borrowed from `tally_span_switch_triplet_variety`. A sequence that reuses the same few chunks scores low. Strict alternation like HTHTHTHT uses only HTH and THT, and a repeating motif like HHTHHT uses only three chunks. This lets the ideal switching rate sit higher without favouring regular high-switch sequences. It targets four critiques that survive the FDR correction:\n- people choose the more-switching sequence among high-switch pairs more often than the model predicts;\n- they choose sequences with a repeating period-3 or period-4 motif less often;\n- at equal switch counts, they choose the sequence with more distinct three-flip chunks more often;\n- it should also help with perfect alternators, which people reject more than predicted (that one just misses the FDR cut).\n\n**Two warnings from the check:**\n- **Convergence wasn't verified**, because a one-chain fit can't report R-hat. Admission's full fit decides that.\n- **The ELPD-LOO estimate is flagged unreliable**: 10% of trials have high Pareto k, well over the 1% allowed. Since the model is the incumben